Fuel: carbs + fluids beforehand, gel ~mile 6.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W35"}	## Headline\nBig week — 42.5 mi, a 15-miler, and your Thursday tempo splits (7:39–7:47) are sharp. But sleep averaged 6.4 hrs, work stress was high, you missed a strength session, and Saturday's easy run "felt like work." That's your body asking for a small step back, not another build. W35 is a light consolidation week: same 5-run shape, mileage trimmed to ~36 mi, Thursday rotates to tempo/MP as planned, and the long run pulls back to 13 with some marathon-pace feel at the end.\n\n## Concerns / flags\n- **Load + life stress combo.** Relative effort has climbed three weeks running (217 → 272 → 342). On its own that's fine; stacked with 6.4 hr sleep and a work deadline it's the exact setup where shins start grumbling. Your W34 target was ~40 mi — you ran 42.5. We're pulling back a touch this week on purpose.\n- **Long-run drift +7.3 bpm again.** That's heat tax more than fitness. Keep the HR 148 cap on the easy portion; the MP finish is by effort, not watch.\n- **Saturday felt like work.** Not a red flag, just a cue. If next Saturday feels the same after an easier week, tell me.\n\n## Your question — handling travel weeks\nSimple rules of thumb:\n1. **Protect the long run and one quality session.** If you can only do 3 runs that week, make them Sunday long, Thursday quality, and one easy midweek.\n2. **Drop easy miles first, not workouts.** The aerobic base is forgiving over 7–10 days.\n3. **Cap the travel week around 70–75% of normal mileage** and treat the following Monday as a true rest, not a catch-up day. No doubling back to "make up" miles.\n4. **If a hotel treadmill is all you have**, easy miles only — no quality on a strange treadmill.\nSend me the dates of your next travel week when you know them and I'll pre-build around it.\n\n## This week — the "why"\n- **Mileage ~36 mi (down from 42.5).** Deliberate absorption week after three straight builds.\n- **Thursday = tempo/MP** per the rotation. 4 × 1 mi at MP (8:00/mi) with short float recoveries — introduces goal pace in bite-sized chunks before we extend it on long runs.\n- **Sunday long = 13 mi**, last 2 at MP effort. Shorter than last Sunday on purpose; quality over distance this week.\n- **Easy days properly easy** — HR under 150. If Tuesday still feels like a slog, cut it to 4 mi, no ego.\n- **Both strength sessions** this week since you're not travelling. The one you skipped is the shin insurance.	\N	2026-08-23 20:00:43.100705+00	2026-08-23 20:00:43.100705+00	[{"body": "Three weeks of rising training load stacked with 6.4 hr sleep and high work stress is a known setup for overuse niggles — especially given your shin history. The pullback this week is intentional. If anything shin-related starts whispering (soreness that lingers after warm-up, tenderness to touch along the bone), stop running and see a sports physio or your doctor before pushing on. Don't try to train through it.", "title": "Watch the stress + sleep combo", "severity": "caution"}]
785715c9-e1fa-4a05-a805-9fc07672efaa	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-14", "notes": "Travel day + full rest. Gentle calf stretching 2× (straight-knee and bent-knee, 30s × 3 each side). Book the physio/sports-med appointment today if you haven't.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "~9:45–10:00/mi, HR ≤145", "distanceM": 4828, "structure": "Only if the morning Achilles check passes. 3 mi very easy on the flat canal — no hills, no strides. Abort immediately if sharp pain or if you catch yourself favoring the leg. If check fails: 30 min easy stationary bike instead."}, "date": "2026-09-15", "notes": "This is a test run, not a training run. Finish feeling like you could have done more.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "~9:45/mi, HR ≤148", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "Only if morning check passes AND Tuesday ran clean. 4 mi flat and easy. Stop if the Achilles sharpens at any point."}, "date": "2026-09-16", "notes": "If Tuesday wasn't clean, swap to 30–40 min bike or pool run.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"date": "2026-09-17", "notes": "Deliberately rest today — this slot was tempo/MP in the old rotation. Not this week. Optional 30 min easy bike or pool run if you want aerobic time. No running.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"date": "2026-09-18", "notes": "Travel day — full rest. Keep up calf stretching.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "~9:50/mi, HR ≤148", "distanceM": 6437, "structure": "4 mi flat and easy, pre-'long' shakeout. Morning check must pass. Abort on any sharp pain."}, "date": "2026-09-19", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "By feel, HR ≤148", "distanceM": 12875, "structure": "UP TO 8 mi, flat, by feel. Not the planned 17. No MP, no strong finish, no hills. If the Achilles starts talking in the first 2 miles, turn around and come home — that's the right answer, not a failure. Fuel practice is still welcome (gel ~mile 4) since it costs the Achilles nothing."}, "date": "2026-09-20", "notes": "Goal of this run: finish with the Achilles no worse than it started. If physio says shorter, do shorter.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W38"}	## Headline\nWe're pulling the handbrake this week. Your right Achilles went from "tight" during Thursday's tempo (sharp enough to stop at mile 2 and walk home) to stiff on Saturday to stiff-after-sitting on Sunday. That pattern — stiffness that eases with movement but keeps showing up — is a classic Achilles warning sign, and the smart move 9 weeks out from Lakeshore is to back off *now*, get it looked at by a qualified sports physio or sports-med doc, and protect the block. One cautious week costs you nothing. A stubborn Achilles pushed through costs you the race.\n\n## Answers to your questions\n**1. Should I stop running for a few days, or keep going with easy runs?**\nShort answer: a few true easy days are okay *if* the Achilles passes a simple morning check — but no quality, no long run as planned, and we abort any run the moment it talks back. The specific test each morning:\n- Can you walk down the stairs normally without a sharp pinch?\n- Can you do 10 single-leg calf raises on the right side without sharp pain (dull "I feel it" is fine, sharp is not)?\n- Is morning stiffness the same or less than the day before?\n\nIf yes to all three → short, flat, slow easy run is on. If no to any → that day becomes rest or a 30-min bike/pool session instead. No negotiating.\n\n**2. How do I know if this is serious?**\nI'm a coach, not a clinician, so I can't diagnose this — and that's exactly why I want you to see a sports physio or sports-med doctor this week, ideally in the next couple of days. The signs that mean *stop running and get seen urgently*: sharp pain that makes you limp or change your gait, visible swelling or a lump on the tendon, a feeling of weakness pushing off, pain that wakes you at night, or anything that feels like a "pop." Getting hands on it early is the single best thing you can do for your race.\n\n## What I'm changing this week\n- **No tempo, no MP, no strides, no hills.** Quality is off the table until the Achilles is quiet and a professional has cleared you.\n- **Long run is cut to 8 miles max, flat, by feel.** Not 17 (which is where the ramp was headed). We don't lose fitness in one easy week — we lose it over three injured weeks.\n- **Mileage ~18–22 mi** (down from 31). All Brooks Ghost, no Endorphin Speed (carbon plate + aggressive geometry is not what a cranky Achilles wants right now).\n- **Two optional cross-training swaps** (bike or pool) if the morning check fails.\n- **Daily calf care**: gentle calf stretching (straight + bent knee) 2× day, and if your physio approves, slow eccentric heel drops are the gold-standard homework — but let them prescribe those, not me.\n\nThe goal this week is simple: finish Sunday with the Achilles quieter than Monday. If we get that, next week we start carefully reintroducing structure. If we don't, we extend the easy block another week. We will not do another real quality session until this is settled.\n\nSend me a quick note mid-week on how Tuesday and Wednesday feel, and tell me what the physio says.	{"Pause Thursday hills/tempo rotation until Achilles is cleared by a clinician and symptom-free for 7+ days","Shelve Saucony Endorphin Speed 4 until Achilles symptoms resolve; daily miles in Brooks Ghost 16 only","Long run governed by symptoms this week — distance ceiling 8 mi, no MP finishes until further notice","Add a daily morning Achilles check (stairs / single-leg calf raise / stiffness trend) as the go/no-go for each run","Revisit the 3:30 goal after we have 2 clean weeks post-clearance — may need to adjust goal or race plan"}	2026-09-13 20:00:57.179123+00	2026-09-13 20:00:57.179123+00	[{"body": "The pattern you're describing — sharp pain mid-tempo that forced you to stop, stiffness the next morning, and stiffness after sitting on Sunday — is something a qualified sports physio or sports-medicine doctor should look at hands-on, ideally in the next few days. I can't diagnose it and I don't want to guess. Getting it assessed early is the single best thing you can do to protect the Lakeshore block.", "title": "Right Achilles — please get this evaluated this week", "severity": "caution"}, {"body": "Stop running and contact a clinician the same day if any of these appear: sharp pain that changes your gait or makes you limp, visible swelling or a lump along the tendon, weakness pushing off the right foot, a sudden 'pop' or snap, or pain that wakes you at night. Don't push through any of those to finish a run.", "title": "Red flags — stop running and seek care promptly", "severity": "urgent"}, {"body": "Each morning before running: (1) walk down stairs normally — no sharp pinch; (2) 10 single-leg calf raises on the right side without sharp pain; (3) morning stiffness the same or better than yesterday. If any check fails, that day becomes rest or 30 minutes of easy bike/pool instead of running. No ego runs this week.", "title": "Daily go/no-go check before any run this week", "severity": "info"}]
a5660788-041e-4bad-b5fa-c3ee40b81b12	{"days": [{"date": "2026-09-21", "notes": "Travel day — full rest. Morning Achilles check: stairs + single-leg calf raise. Note anything that isn't neutral.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "~9:30/mi, HR ≤148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi conversational on the flat canal path. First mile deliberately slow — this is also the Achilles warm-up test. If anything beyond mild first-half-mile tightness, cut short and tell me."}, "date": "2026-09-22", "notes": "Go/no-go = morning Achilles check. Properly easy.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "20–30 min: slow heel drops (straight + bent knee, 3×15 each side), single-leg calf raises, glute bridges, side plank"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "Z2, HR ≤148", "distanceM": 9656, "structure": "6 mi steady aerobic on the canal. Keep HR ≤148 — if it drifts into Z3 by mile 4, back off pace. Don't chase last Wednesday's splits."}, "date": "2026-09-23", "notes": "Mid-week aerobic anchor. HR is the governor.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "~9:30/mi, HR ≤148", "distanceM": 8047, "structure": "5 mi easy on the FLAT canal — not the hilly route. No strides, no pickups. Just honest aerobic miles."}, "date": "2026-09-24", "notes": "Normally the quality day. Stays easy and flat until Achilles is cleared by a clinician and we've got 7+ fully clean days.", "dayType": "easy"}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "notes": "Travel day — full rest.", "dayType": "rest"}, {"run": {"type": "easy", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "~9:40/mi, HR ≤145", "distanceM": 4828, "structure": "3 mi truly easy pre-long shakeout. Short on purpose."}, "date": "2026-09-26", "notes": "Short shakeout. If anything isn't right, swap for rest.", "dayType": "easy", "strength": "20–30 min: heel drops, single-leg calf raises, core — the Achilles/shin insurance session"}, {"run": {"type": "long", "shoes": "Brooks Ghost 16", "paceOrHr": "HR ≤148 avg; pace is the output", "distanceM": 16093, "structure": "10 mi long run by HR. Cap average HR at 148. No MP finish this week. If anything in the Achilles flares during the run — tightness that doesn't ease by mile 2, any sharpness, any limp — stop and walk home. Fuel: gel pre-run and one at mile 5, water throughout."}, "date": "2026-09-27", "notes": "Modest extension from last week's 8. The win is a clean run, not a fast one.", "dayType": "long"}], "week": "2026-W39"}	## Headline\nReally encouraging week. You held the easy-only guardrails, built back to 26 mi across 5 runs, and the Achilles went from "slight tightness first half mile" Tue → "a hint at the start" Wed → "fine on the flat" Thu → nothing Sat or Sun, no post-run stiffness. That's the trajectory we wanted. We're now 8 weeks out from Lakeshore (Nov 15). This week we build cautiously — still easy-only, Ghost 16 only, daily morning Achilles check as the go/no-go, and we nudge the long run from 8 to 10. No hills, no tempo, no MP touches yet.\n\n## Concerns / flags\n- **Still no clinician check-in on record.** The W38 note was explicit: hills/tempo rotation stays paused until you've been cleared by a clinician AND been symptom-free for 7+ days. You've got a strong week of symptom-free running, but I don't want to add intensity on the back of self-clearance alone. See the callout — please get it looked at this week if you haven't.\n- **Easy pace creeping down** (9:34 → 9:30 → 9:23 → 9:24/mi at HR 143–144). Good aerobic sign, but on a healing Achilles I'd rather you stay honest-easy and let pace be the output. Don't chase Wednesday's 9:15s.\n- **Wednesday run drifted to Z3** (15.7% Z3, avg HR 148). Fine in isolation but it's the day easy becomes "steady." Keep HR ≤148 this Wednesday.\n- Sleep 7.5, strength 2/2, stress low — all green.\n\n## Your questions\n**1. When can I do a hard session again?**\nThe gate is unchanged: clinician clearance + 7 consecutive symptom-free days (no stiffness on morning stairs, no tightness in the first mile, nothing after the run). You've just completed what looks like your first clean week — so the earliest a quality session comes back is W40, and only if W39 is also clean AND you've got clearance. When we re-introduce, it'll be a soft return: a short strides-only session or a 2 × 1 mi at MP, not straight back into hills or a 4-mile tempo.\n\n**2. Does the missed week change what's realistic for race day?**\nHonest answer: probably, yes. We're 8 weeks out and we've lost the Thursday quality rotation for ~3 weeks. 3:30 required MP work compounded over months — the fitness you built through W36 (that MP block at 7:58/mi) is still in the bank, but we need 4–5 clean MP sessions and 2–3 big long runs with MP finishes to sharpen it, and we don't have room for all of that now. My read: **3:35–3:38 is a realistic A-goal if the next two weeks are clean and quality returns in W40**, with 3:30 moved to a stretch "if the day is perfect" target. Let's make the call formally after W40 — I need to see one quality session back on the legs before I commit to a race plan.\n\n## This week — the "why"\n- **~31 mi, all easy, Ghost 16 only.** +5 mi on last week is a sensible step; we're not rushing back to 40.\n- **Thursday stays flat and easy** (not the hilly route). Hilly route returns only when quality returns.\n- **Long run to 10 mi**, by HR, no MP finish. Within last week's ceiling philosophy but a modest extension because you had zero symptoms Sat/Sun.\n- **Daily morning Achilles check stays in** as the go/no-go. If stairs or single-leg calf raise are stiff/painful, swap that day for rest and tell me.\n- **Strength both sessions** — calf raises and slow heel drops are directly protective here.\n- **Wednesday stays honest-easy** (HR ≤148, slow down if needed).	{"Keep Thursday quality rotation paused through W39; earliest return is W40 and only with clinician clearance + 7 clean days","Keep Endorphin Speed 4 shelved; Ghost 16 only until quality returns","Formally revisit 3:30 goal after W40 — current working range 3:35–3:38 pending clean build","Keep daily morning Achilles check as the per-run go/no-go"}	2026-09-20 20:00:58.815011+00	2026-09-20 20:00:58.815011+00	[{"body": "Last week was symptom-free and that's great news, but I want a qualified clinician (sports physio or sports med doc) to actually assess the Achilles before we put hills, tempo or marathon-pace work back on your plate. I can coach the running load; I can't evaluate the tendon. Please book that appointment this week if you haven't. Bring the W37 tempo-run notes with you.", "title": "Get the Achilles looked at before quality returns", "severity": "caution"}, {"body": "Walk home and message me if any of these show up: sharp pain in the Achilles, pain that makes you change your stride or limp, tightness that doesn't ease within the first mile, or swelling/warmth you can see or feel afterward. These are escalate-to-clinician signals, not push-through signals.", "title": "Stop signals during any run this week", "severity": "caution"}]
