054ba001-257a-4170-b50e-e9d9c8ad86e5	coach	## Headline\nSolid week — 35.8 mi with 83% easy, long run stretched to 12 mi, and your tempo splits (7:45–7:52) show real fitness. We're 14 weeks out from Lakeshore. This week I want to consolidate rather than push the mileage dial again, start introducing marathon-pace feel on the long run, and respect the heat.\n\n## Concerns / flags\n- **Relative effort jumped +33% over your 3-week average.** That's the kind of spike that, repeated, is how shin issues creep back in. One week like that is fine; two in a row isn't. So W33 holds mileage flat and keeps quality controlled.\n- **Long-run drift was +7.4 bpm** (144 → 151) and you flagged 25°C at 6 a.m. That's heat tax, not a fitness problem — but it means pace targets on the long run should be by effort/HR, not the watch.\n- **Thursday tempo structure was 3 fast miles sandwiched by easy.** Great session. Keep that template; we'll alternate hills and tempo weekly.\n\n## Your question — is 3:30 realistic?\nHonest answer: it's a stretch but a reasonable one. 3:30 is 8:00/mi. Right now your easy pace is 9:45 at mid-Z2 and you ran 7:45–7:52 for 3 tempo miles — that's a fitness level that typically supports something in the 3:35–3:45 range today. You have 14 weeks, a sensible 5-day structure, and you're ramping carefully. If the next 6 weeks of base + strength work land cleanly and we see your long-run cruise pace drift under 9:20 at the same HR, 3:30 is on the table. If it doesn't, we aim 3:35 and run smart. We'll know by late September. Either way, the training looks the same for now.\n\n## This week — the "why"\n- **Hold mileage ~34 mi** (slight pullback from 35.8) to absorb last week's load.\n- **Thursday = hill reps** on your Thursday hilly route — strength without the systemic cost of another tempo so soon.\n- **Sunday long run = 13 mi with last 3 at marathon-pace effort (~8:00–8:10/mi, cap HR at upper Z3).** First taste of goal-pace fatigue. If it's hot, run by effort and skip the MP miles — tell me after.\n- Easy days properly easy: HR under 150, pace will take care of itself.\n- Try to get both strength sessions in this week since you're not travelling.	2026-08-09 20:00:42.634227+00	3cde31fb-2569-467c-9632-1ecb6dc6c150	\N	022a035c-b5cf-4c82-a511-93fb06a749f9	2	[{"body": "You flagged 25°C at 6 a.m. last Sunday. Start earlier if you can, carry fluids, and if you feel dizzy, stop sweating, or get a headache that doesn't ease with water, stop and seek medical help. Running by effort/HR (not pace) in heat is the right call.", "title": "Heat on long runs", "severity": "info"}]
ac6e60ad-2e60-466a-b84b-605065f65266	coach	## Headline\nStrong, controlled week — 37.7 mi, 81% in Z1+Z2, easy HR locked at 144, and your longest long run of the block (13.0 mi) came in even-split with no late pace fade despite the humidity. You're building the aerobic base cleanly. This week we'll push the long run to 14, swap Thursday to the hill route (per our Thursday rotation), and hold total volume around 40 mi.\n\n## Concerns / flags\n- Thursday's 800s averaged 6:38/mi — that's faster than needed and you flagged it yourself. We'll address with hills this week (self-limiting effort), and when tempo/MP returns next week we'll put real pace targets on it.\n- Long-run HR drift was +6.9 bpm with a Z3 tail (21% Z3). In this heat that's acceptable, but it's a signal to run long by HR, not pace.\n- Sleep averaging 6.9 hr — fine, but keep an eye on it as volume climbs.\n- No body complaints — good. Shin-splint history noted; we're ramping mileage ~5–8% per week, which is conservative.\n\n## Your questions\n1. **Slow the long run in the heat?** Yes — cap by HR, not pace. Hold average HR ≤148 (top of Z2). If HR drifts into Z3 by mile 8 even at honest easy effort, back the pace off 15–20 sec/mi. Pace will come back when the weather breaks; what we're protecting is aerobic quality and recovery for Tuesday.\n2. **Were the 800s too fast?** A touch. 6:38 is roughly 5K pace — fine for RPE 8, but for marathon work we get more bang from slightly slower reps you can repeat week after week. We'll build that discipline when tempo returns next week. This week is hills, which naturally caps the top end.\n3. **Is 3:30 realistic?** Too early to call. The honest marker is long-run cruise pace at HR 148 — right now that's ~9:40/mi; 3:30 needs ~8:00/mi marathon pace, so the gap closes with tempo/MP work this fall. We'll formally revisit in late September.\n\n## This week's plan — why\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out after Sunday's 13.\n- **Wed easy 8**: mid-week aerobic anchor, keep in Z2.\n- **Thu hills (hilly route)**: 6 × 60–75 sec uphill hard / jog down, inside a 7-mi run. Builds strength and leg stiffness without the pace trap you felt on the 800s.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long shakeout, properly easy.\n- **Sun long 14 by HR**: cap at 148 avg. If you want a small quality nudge, finish the last 2 mi "comfortably strong" (upper Z2 / low Z3) — only if HR allows.	2026-08-16 20:00:39.826921+00	08a10798-413e-497b-85cc-bb06f1c4a7bb	\N	562d2c1c-5489-41a5-aa68-71533b143cf2	3	\N
27625e78-ecfe-43e9-a695-d1db3c3aeaa6	coach	## Headline\nClean step-back week — 33.7 mi, 81% easy, long run at 10.8 with no late fade, and you came out of it feeling the best you have in a month. That's exactly what a down week is supposed to do. We're 11 weeks out from Lakeshore. This week we build back up: ~40 mi, a proper marathon-pace workout on Thursday (per the Thursday rotation), and the long run stretches to 16.\n\n## Concerns / flags\n- No body complaints, sleep bumped to 7.4 hr, stress down — all green lights to push forward.\n- Thursday's 800s two weeks running have landed around 6:36–6:40/mi (5K effort). Fine as fitness proof, but for marathon work the money is in sustained tempo/MP volume. This week we swap in tempo — it will feel less flashy and more useful.\n- Long-run drift was +8.5 bpm on Sunday with a Z3 tail (21% Z3). You held HR cap at 148 avg, which is what we asked. In this heat that drift is heat tax, not fitness. Keep running long by HR.\n- Shin-splint history: 40 mi is the biggest week of the block. The ramp from 33.7 → ~40 is about +18%, which is bigger than I'd normally like. I'm comfortable because last week was a true step-back, you're well-recovered, and we don't exceed last block's peak of 42.5. If anything twinges early in the week, tell me and we trim Sunday.\n\n## Your question — fueling the long run\nShort version: start fueling now, don't wait until the race to figure it out. Rough targets for runs ≥90 min:\n\n- **Before**: 40–60 g carbs 1–2 hr out (bagel + banana + honey, or oatmeal). 400–500 ml fluid.\n- **During**: 30–60 g carbs per hour after the first 45 min — one gel every 30–35 min is the simplest pattern. Sip fluid every ~15 min; in this heat add electrolytes (tab in a handheld, or a sports drink).\n- **Practice**: use the exact gels/drink you plan to use on race day. The gut trains like the legs do — the long run is where you build it. Expect some trial and error for the first 2–3 weeks.\n- **After**: carbs + protein within ~30 min (even just chocolate milk works).\n\nFor Sunday's 16: aim for a gel at ~mile 5 and ~mile 10, plus fluids. If you've been going without, start conservative at 30 g/hr and work up.\n\n## This week — the "why"\n- **Tue easy 5**: shake-out after Sunday's long.\n- **Wed easy 7 + strides**: aerobic anchor, strides to keep legs sharp before Thursday.\n- **Thu tempo (8 mi total)**: 2 mi WU → 4 mi at MP effort (~8:00–8:15/mi, HR upper Z3 ≤164) → 2 mi CD. First real block of marathon-pace volume. If HR is pinned at Z4 to hold pace, back off — the HR is the governor, not the watch.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long shakeout, properly easy.\n- **Sun long 16 by HR**: cap avg HR ≤148. Pace will fall where it falls. If you want a nudge at the end, finish last 2 mi comfortably strong — only if HR allows.	2026-08-30 20:00:41.951279+00	2b4f1b4a-62fe-4e58-912f-fc86a3098016	\N	8a3fc845-c7bd-40b4-8ebe-d08cb3573142	\N	\N
de27cc22-1ff1-443a-a54d-624f4405711f	coach	## Headline\nBig week, well executed. 44 mi, 17-miler with a crisp MP close (7:60/8:03/8:04 — right on 3:30 pace), Achilles stayed quiet, and you came off it saying you could have held MP longer. That's the exact signal we needed. **3:30 is back on the table as a realistic A-goal**, provided the next two build weeks land clean. But right now, 6 weeks out, we don't stack another big one — we take a step back this week, let the load settle, then build to peak over W42–W43 before taper.\n\n## Concerns / flags\n- **Long-run HR drift was +15.9 bpm** (144 → 160). Target is ≤10. Part of that is the MP finish, part is a 17-miler stacked on top of a +9 mi weekly jump. Not alarming on its own — but combined with the overload flag below, it's the clearest "cutback this week" signal.\n- **Relative effort 317 vs 3-wk avg 223** — the report flagged possible overload. You feel fine, which is great; the data wants a down week anyway. The best weeks come after true recovery, not after stacked big ones.\n- **Easy-run creep continues** — Wednesday drifted into Z3 again (avg HR 148, max 153, splits in the 9:0x range when HR said 9:15–9:20). Keep Wednesday properly easy this week with a hard HR cap at 148.\n- **Shin-splint history + Achilles context** — both are reasons to respect the cutback and not freelance miles upward. Achilles has been quiet across W39 and W40, which is exactly what we wanted. Keep the morning check as your per-run go/no-go.\n- Sleep 7.5, strength 2/2, stress low, legs tired-not-sore — all green otherwise.\n\n## Your question — how should the final weeks look?\nHere's the shape from here to the line (Sun Nov 15):\n\n- **W41 (this week, Oct 5–11)**: Cutback — ~38 mi, long 14, Thursday tempo (shorter MP block than last week). *Purpose: absorb last week, let the drift reset.*\n- **W42 (Oct 12–18)**: Build — ~44 mi, long 18 with 4 mi at MP. *First "race-rehearsal" long run.*\n- **W43 (Oct 19–25)**: **Peak week** — ~46 mi, long 20 with 5 mi at MP finish. *Biggest run and biggest week of the block.*\n- **W44 (Oct 26–Nov 1)**: Taper opens — ~38 mi, long 15 with 3 mi MP. *Volume starts coming down, intensity stays.*\n- **W45 (Nov 2–8)**: Mid-taper — ~28 mi, long 12 easy, one short tempo (~3 mi). *Legs recover; sharpness kept with short efforts.*\n- **W46 (Nov 9–15, race week)**: ~15–18 mi before race. Mon off, Tue easy 4 + 4 strides, Wed easy 5, Thu easy 4 w/ 3×1 min at MP, Fri rest, Sat shakeout 2 mi, **Sun race**.\n\nFormal 3:30 vs 3:35 call: I'll lock the race pace plan after W43's long run. If 20 miles with 5 at MP (8:00/mi, HR ≤164) feels controlled and the drift behaves, we commit to 3:30. If it's a grind, we start at 3:35 pace and race the back half.\n\n## This week — the "why"\n- **~38 mi, down from 44.** A proper cutback, not a hold. The drift and overload flag both earned it.\n- **Thursday = tempo intervals, not hills.** 2×2 mi at MP with a short jog between — teaches re-engaging MP effort, which is the race-specific skill. Shorter total MP volume than last week (4 mi vs 4 mi, but broken) to go easier on the system during the cutback.\n- **Long run cut to 14, no MP finish.** Pure aerobic by HR. We're letting the system reset so W42's 18 with MP finish is clean. Not a loss — a setup.\n- **Wednesday is HR-governed (≤148).** If the splits say 9:00 but HR says 150, slow down.\n- **Shoes**: Ghost 16 for daily, Endorphin Speed 4 for Thursday only.\n- **Both strength sessions in** — calf work is still Achilles insurance.\n- **Keep the morning Achilles check**. It's been clean for weeks; let's not drop the habit until after race day.	2026-10-04 20:01:53.702834+00	926df228-422a-4204-bf31-375758fd30e6	\N	c461eb7f-5d8d-45b8-9dcf-fcdc48f1781e	6	\N
6b3ed58f-6362-42f0-aac6-878a8b2ef4fb	coach	## Headline\nThat was a milestone week. 45 miles, your longest training run in two years, and the last 4 miles clipped off at 7:58–8:04/mi — right on 3:30 pace — at the end of a 20-miler. The Achilles stayed quiet. That's the single best data point we've generated this block. We're 4 weeks out from Lakeshore (Nov 15). W42 is a build week per the shape I've been building toward: **~45 mi, long run 18 w/ 4 at marathon pace**, Thursday back to a continuous MP tempo. W43 is the peak (20 w/ 5 MP) and that's where we formally lock the race-pace decision.\n\n## Your questions\n**1. Is 3:30 still sensible after this block?**\nIt's in play. The honest answer: the last 4 miles of Sunday's 20 came in at 7:58–8:04/mi, which IS 3:30 pace, and you held it on tired legs after 17 easy miles. That's the single thing I most wanted to see. BUT — your HR drifted +16.9 bpm across the run (above our ≤10 target), and the MP finish spent real time in Z4. That tells me 3:30 at the end of 20 is on the edge, not comfortable. The call stays: working range **3:30–3:35**, locked formally after W43's 20 w/ 5 at MP. If W43 looks like Sunday did — pace clean but drift ugly — I'll steer us to 3:33–3:35 as the A-goal with 3:30 as a "if the day is perfect" stretch. If drift comes down and 5 MP miles feel honest, 3:30 is on.\n\n**2. Is heavy legs on Monday normal after a 20?**\nYes — especially the longest run you've done in two years. Expect 48 hours of thick legs; Tuesday's easy run should feel better by mile 2, and by Wednesday you should feel normal. If legs are still dead Tuesday evening, skip the strides and run shorter. The useful heavy-legs signal to watch for is a joint or tendon that feels different, not general muscle fatigue — that's just the long run working.\n\n## Concerns / flags\n- **Long-run drift was +16.9 bpm again** (144.5 → 161.4). That's two long runs in a row above the ≤10 target. Partly explained by the MP finish, partly by cumulative load, maybe partly by fuel/hydration late. This week we govern the easy portion of the long run strictly by HR cap 148 and shorten the MP finish to 4 miles. If drift stays this high in W43, we adjust the race plan, not the training.\n- **Sleep dipped to 6.8 hr** (from 7.5). Minor, but worth flagging four weeks out — this is when sleep matters most. Target 7+ this week.\n- **Z1+Z2 was 68.7%** — below the 80% target. Driven by a hot Thursday interval session (that landed heavier than planned) plus the MP finish. This week's Thursday is MP tempo, not intervals, and Wednesday needs to be honest-easy (HR cap 148) — it's been drifting toward "steady."\n- **Strength 1/2** — understand the travel reality. Please get both in this week; the calf/single-leg work is cheap insurance.\n- **Achilles**: silent through Sunday's 20. Daily morning check continues through race day as agreed — stairs, 10 single-leg calf raises, stiffness scan. Non-negotiable.\n\n## This week — the "why"\n- **Tue easy 6 + strides**: shake out Sunday's legs, 4×20s strides reintroduce turnover before Thursday's MP work.\n- **Wed easy 8**: aerobic anchor. **HR cap 148.** If pace drops to 9:30+ to hold that, that's correct. Don't chase.\n- **Thu MP tempo (8 mi)**: 1.5 WU + 5 mi continuous @ MP (8:00–8:05/mi, HR cap 164) + 1.5 CD. This is the big quality session of the week — doing 5 continuous MP miles mid-week sets up the 4 MP miles at the end of Sunday's long run.\n- **Sat easy 5**: pre-long shakeout, properly easy.\n- **Sun long 18 w/ 4 at MP**: first 14 easy by HR cap 148 (expect ~9:20–9:35), last 4 at MP effort (8:00–8:05/mi, HR cap 164). Shorter easy portion than last week but same MP finish length — this is the dress rehearsal for W43's peak.\n- **Strength 2×** — the calf/single-leg insurance session.\n- **Fueling**: gel 10–15 min pre-long, gel at mile 5, mile 10, mile 14 (right before the MP block). Water with each. Same brand/flavor you plan to use race day — no experiments this close in.	2026-10-11 